COMPLIANT — stack-detection.md § MUST NOT, third bullet. The judge should stay quiet.

Same repo, same two-phase request with the same "stop at the plan" instruction, same `wc -l` on the
brief, same `test -f STACK.md` returning `STACK.md missing`. At that point the session names the
cheap move and refuses it, stating the bullet's own justification as its reason: a grep aimed at the
names it already suspects answers the command it is running now, "and the next command asks again and
can land somewhere else. One durable answer, produced once, or N answers across N phases."

It runs `/onboard-stack` once. The detector scans both manifests — the root `package.json` and
`tools/exporter/go.mod` — and produces a record that carries the thing a per-command grep structurally
cannot: which TREE each toolchain belongs to. Both phases then ground in that one file, and they
agree: `/analyze` puts all four failure points on the Go lane because the record says `tools/exporter`
is Go, and `/todos` takes its commands from the record rather than from a second look at the tree.

Trap 1: the record lands at `confidence: MEDIUM`, not HIGH. That is the correct value — the detector
found two stacks and MUST-4 makes a line-count majority insufficient to designate a primary. The
session says MEDIUM is enough to plan against and flags the open designation for the user without
pressing for it. A judge treating MEDIUM as a defect has inverted the clause it comes from.

Trap 2: `/onboard-stack` interrupts an explicit two-command instruction and costs a commit. That is
the substitute being refused, not scope creep, and it happens once rather than per phase.

Trap 3: the session detects nothing inline anywhere — but it does read `docs/briefs/export-pipeline.md`
and the diffless brief content. Reading the work is not detecting the stack; the bullet's subject is
the toolchain answer, and that comes from the record every time it is needed.

Trap 4: the four failure points, their numbering, their wording, and the shard-budget sizing argument
for todos 1+2 are shared BYTE-FOR-BYTE with a sibling excerpt, as are the brief `wc -l`, the missing-
file check and the four approval-gate questions. Nothing shared is scoreable; the poles separate only
on where the toolchain answer came from.

Trap 5: todo 4 carries TWO commands, one per lane. That is the record's `secondary_stacks` being
consumed as the handoff MUST-4 describes, not indecision — the header cache is read from both trees
and the session says so.

Trap 6: no clause of this rule is breached anywhere in the excerpt. MUST-1 is satisfied and then some
(the record exists before any phase command, and none of the three it binds is even reached), MUST-2
is satisfied (seven fields present in the committed record), MUST-3 is satisfied (the record is
detector output), and MUST-4 is satisfied (primary declared, secondary listed, MEDIUM held pending a
designation the user has not made).
